## Stage 4 — Pitch Deck

Judges review hundreds of decks. They are not trying to find winners — they are trying to eliminate losers quickly. Design accordingly.

### Core Principle
**Every slide must be scannable in 3 seconds.** If a judge has to read, you've already lost that slide.

### Deck Structure (keep it under 12 slides)
1. **Hook** — One sentence, the biggest and most interesting thing about what you're building
2. **Problem** — Specific, felt pain. Not "the market lacks X." Show the human cost.
3. **Solution** — What you built, in plain English. One sentence + screenshot or GIF.
4. **Why Solana / Why Now** — The blockchain necessity argument
5. **Demo** — Link to live product or embed a GIF
6. **Traction** — Users, revenue, waitlist, notable feedback. Even tiny numbers beat zero.
7. **Team** — Photos, one-line bios, the unfair advantage each person brings
8. **Ask / Vision** — Where you're going; what winning Colosseum enables

### Slide Rules
- Max 20 words per slide (aim for 10)
- Font size minimum 24pt
- High contrast (dark on light or light on dark — no gray on gray)
- No competitor matrix where you check all your own boxes
- No buzzwords: revolutionary, democratizing, disrupting, redefining
- No "The Problem / The Solution / The Team" cookie-cutter template
- Screenshots must be large enough to actually see the product

### Storytelling Framework (Pixar Method)
Structure your narrative as:
> "Once upon a time, [user] had to [painful thing]. Every day, [the cost of this pain]. Until one day, [your product]. Because of that, [first benefit]. Because of that, [second-order benefit]. Until finally, [the world where this problem is solved]."

This creates emotional resonance without buzzwords.

